module

public import StacksFrontier.CategoryTheory.DifferentialGraded.RightModule.DerivedEvaluation
public import StacksFrontier.CategoryTheory.DifferentialGraded.RightModule.EvaluationReflector
public import StacksFrontier.CategoryTheory.Triangulated.LocalizingReflectorKernel

/-!
# The derived Hom functor with values in `D(E)`, and its unit

Let `C` be a differential graded category over a commutative ring `R`, let `K` be an object of
`C` and let `E = C(K, K)` be the one-object differential graded subcategory on `K`. This file
assembles steps 1 and 2 of the replacement proof of [Stacks, Tag 09LK] into a functor

`R̃ : D(A) ⥤ D(E)`

out of the derived category of the source, together with a natural transformation
`Hom_dg(K, -) ⟶ R̃ ∘ q` out of the homotopy-category-level Hom functor. These are the two data
that step 3 consumes.

## Which composite the proof actually needs

Step 1 produces an exact reflector `L` on `K(Mod^dg(C))` with unit `η : 𝟭 ⟶ L`, killing the
localizing subcategory `N` generated by the representables `h_B` attached to the acyclic
objects `B` of the source. Step 2 names `R̃ = ev_K ∘ J`. Two different quotients of
`K(Mod^dg(C))` are in play and only one of them is the target of `J`:

* the quotient by the **acyclic** right differential graded modules, which is `D(C)` — this is
  the one evaluation at `K` descends past, by `DGCategory.isAcyclic_evaluation`, giving
  `DGCategory.derivedEvaluation : D(C) ⥤ D(E)`;
* the quotient by **`N`**, which evaluation at `K` does *not* descend past, because
  `ev_K h_X = C(K, X)` is in general neither zero nor acyclic.

So `J` is not the Verdier embedding `A/(A ⊓ N) ⥤ K(Mod^dg(C))/N` of
`DGCategory.RightModule.yonedaVerdierEmbedding`, whose target is the second quotient. It is
the descent of `X ↦ L h_X` followed by the quotient functor of `D(C)`: `L` kills `N`
(`CategoryTheory.ObjectProperty.isZero_of_bijective_precomp_unit`), the representable of an
acyclic object lies in `N`, and so the composite inverts the quasi-isomorphisms of the source
and descends to `J : D(A) ⥤ D(C)`. Then `R̃ = J ⋙ ev_K` with the `ev_K` of `D(C) ⥤ D(E)`, and
this composite *is* `D(E)`-valued on `D(A)`, which is what step 3 asks for.

## The hypotheses, named rather than hidden

The source is kept abstract, as a triangulated category `S` with an exact functor
`Φ : S ⥤ K(Mod^dg(C))` — in the intended reading `S = K(A)` and `Φ` is the differential graded
Yoneda functor `X ↦ h_X` — a triangulated subcategory `Z` of `S` (the acyclic complexes) and a
localization `q : S ⥤ D(A)` of `S` at the morphisms whose cone lies in `Z`. The one hypothesis
relating the two sides is `DGCategory.isZero_comp_obj_of_mem`: the reflector annihilates the
image under `Φ` of every object of `Z`. That is exactly the sentence "`N` is the
localizing subcategory generated by the `h_B` for `B` acyclic", stated as what it is used for.

Nothing here discharges the exactness of `Φ`. For `S = K(A)` and `Φ` the differential graded
Yoneda functor that would need a cone situation on `C` itself and the compatibility of the
Yoneda functor with cones; neither is in this development, and both are hypotheses of the
statements below rather than consequences of them.

## Main definitions

* `DGCategory.derivedReflectorDescent`: the functor `J : D(A) ⥤ D(C)` of step 1.
* `DGCategory.derivedHomReflector`: `R̃ = J ⋙ ev_K : D(A) ⥤ D(E)` of step 2.
* `DGCategory.derivedHomReflectorCompIso`: the isomorphism
  `q ⋙ R̃ ≅ Φ ⋙ L ⋙ ev_K ⋙ Q_E` identifying `R̃` on the image of `q`.
* `DGCategory.derivedHomReflectorUnit`: the natural transformation
  `Φ ⋙ ev_K ⋙ Q_E ⟶ q ⋙ R̃`.

## Main results

* `DGCategory.isZero_derivedQ_comp_obj`: the composite `Φ ⋙ L ⋙ Q_C` kills `Z`, which is what
  makes the descent exist.
* The instances `(derivedReflectorDescent ...).IsTriangulated` and
  `(derivedHomReflector ...).IsTriangulated`: both functors are exact.
* `DGCategory.yonedaOnK_comp_evaluationOnK_comp_derivedQ`: for the differential graded Yoneda
  functor the source of the unit is `Hom_dg(K, -)` of [Stacks, Tag 09LE], read in `D(E)`.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 09LK](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09LK)
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace DGCategory

universe v₂ u₂ v₁ u₁ v u r

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits CategoryTheory.Pretriangulated
open CategoryTheory.ObjectProperty

variable {R : Type r} [CommRing R] (C : DGCategory.{v, u} R) (K : C.Obj)
  (L : (rightModuleCategory.{v} C).K ⥤ (rightModuleCategory.{v} C).K)
  [L.CommShift ℤ] [L.IsTriangulated]
  {S : Type u₁} [Category.{v₁} S] [HasZeroObject S] [HasShift S ℤ] [Preadditive S]
  [∀ n : ℤ, (shiftFunctor S n).Additive] [Pretriangulated S] [IsTriangulated S]
  (Φ : S ⥤ (rightModuleCategory.{v} C).K) [Φ.CommShift ℤ] [Φ.IsTriangulated]
  (Z : ObjectProperty S) [Z.IsTriangulated]
  {DA : Type u₂} [Category.{v₂} DA] [HasZeroObject DA] [HasShift DA ℤ] [Preadditive DA]
  [∀ n : ℤ, (shiftFunctor DA n).Additive] [Pretriangulated DA]
  (q : S ⥤ DA) [q.IsLocalization Z.trW] [q.CommShift ℤ] [q.IsTriangulated]

omit [L.CommShift ℤ] [L.IsTriangulated] [HasZeroObject S] [HasShift S ℤ] [Preadditive S]
  [∀ (n : ℤ), (shiftFunctor S n).Additive] [Pretriangulated S] [IsTriangulated S]
  [Φ.CommShift ℤ] [Φ.IsTriangulated] [Z.IsTriangulated] in
/-- **The annihilation hypothesis, in the form it is used.** If the reflector `L` annihilates a
subcategory `N` of `K(Mod^dg(C))` and the functor `Φ` carries every object of `Z` into `N`,
then `L ∘ Φ` kills `Z`. In the intended reading `Z` is the acyclic complexes, `Φ` the
differential graded Yoneda functor and `N` the localizing subcategory generated by the
representables `h_B` of the acyclic `B`. -/
theorem isZero_comp_obj_of_mem (N : ObjectProperty (rightModuleCategory.{v} C).K)
    (hL : ∀ M, N M → IsZero (L.obj M)) (hZ : ∀ X, Z X → N (Φ.obj X)) (X : S) (hX : Z X) :
    IsZero ((Φ ⋙ L).obj X) :=
  hL _ (hZ X hX)

omit [L.CommShift ℤ] [L.IsTriangulated] [HasZeroObject S] [HasShift S ℤ] [Preadditive S]
  [∀ (n : ℤ), (shiftFunctor S n).Additive] [Pretriangulated S] [IsTriangulated S]
  [Φ.CommShift ℤ] [Φ.IsTriangulated] [Z.IsTriangulated] in
/-- The composite `Φ ⋙ L` followed by the quotient functor of `D(C)` sends every object of `Z`
to a zero object, so it inverts the morphisms of `Z.trW`. -/
theorem isZero_derivedQ_comp_obj (hΦ : ∀ X, Z X → IsZero ((Φ ⋙ L).obj X)) (X : S) (hX : Z X) :
    IsZero ((Φ ⋙ L ⋙ RightModule.derivedQ C).obj X) :=
  Functor.map_isZero (RightModule.derivedQ C) (hΦ X hX)

/-- **The functor `J : D(A) ⥤ D(C)` of step 1 of [Stacks, Tag 09LK].** It is the descent of
`X ↦ L (Φ X)`, followed by the quotient functor of the derived category `D(C)`, through the
localization `q`. Its target is the quotient of `K(Mod^dg(C))` by the acyclic modules, not the
quotient by `N`. -/
noncomputable def derivedReflectorDescent (hΦ : ∀ X, Z X → IsZero ((Φ ⋙ L).obj X)) :
    DA ⥤ RightModule.derivedCategory C :=
  Z.descend q (Φ ⋙ L ⋙ RightModule.derivedQ C) (isZero_derivedQ_comp_obj C L Φ Z hΦ)

/-- `J` is a lifting of `Φ ⋙ L ⋙ Q` along the localization `q` with respect to `Z.trW`. -/
noncomputable instance (hΦ : ∀ X, Z X → IsZero ((Φ ⋙ L).obj X)) :
    Localization.Lifting q Z.trW (Φ ⋙ L ⋙ RightModule.derivedQ C)
      (derivedReflectorDescent C L Φ Z q hΦ) :=
  inferInstanceAs (Localization.Lifting q Z.trW (Φ ⋙ L ⋙ RightModule.derivedQ C)
    (Z.descend q (Φ ⋙ L ⋙ RightModule.derivedQ C) (isZero_derivedQ_comp_obj C L Φ Z hΦ)))

/-- `J` commutes with the shift by `ℤ`, with the structure induced through the localization `q`
from that of `Φ ⋙ L ⋙ Q`. -/
noncomputable instance (hΦ : ∀ X, Z X → IsZero ((Φ ⋙ L).obj X)) :
    (derivedReflectorDescent C L Φ Z q hΦ).CommShift ℤ :=
  Functor.commShiftOfLocalization q Z.trW ℤ (Φ ⋙ L ⋙ RightModule.derivedQ C)
    (derivedReflectorDescent C L Φ Z q hΦ)

/-- **`J` is exact.** -/
instance isTriangulated_derivedReflectorDescent
    (hΦ : ∀ X, Z X → IsZero ((Φ ⋙ L).obj X)) :
    (derivedReflectorDescent C L Φ Z q hΦ).IsTriangulated :=
  Z.isTriangulated_descend q (Φ ⋙ L ⋙ RightModule.derivedQ C)
    (isZero_derivedQ_comp_obj C L Φ Z hΦ)

/-- Restricting `J` along the localization `q` gives back `X ↦ L (Φ X)` in `D(C)`. -/
noncomputable def derivedReflectorDescentCompIso
    (hΦ : ∀ X, Z X → IsZero ((Φ ⋙ L).obj X)) :
    q ⋙ derivedReflectorDescent C L Φ Z q hΦ ≅ Φ ⋙ L ⋙ RightModule.derivedQ C :=
  Z.descendCompIso q (Φ ⋙ L ⋙ RightModule.derivedQ C) (isZero_derivedQ_comp_obj C L Φ Z hΦ)

/-- **`R̃ = ev_K ∘ J : D(A) ⥤ D(E)`**, the exact functor of step 2 of [Stacks, Tag 09LK] with
values in the derived category of right differential graded modules over the endomorphism
complex `E = C(K, K)`. It is `DGCategory.derivedReflectorDescent` followed by evaluation at
`K` on the derived categories, `DGCategory.derivedEvaluation`. -/
noncomputable def derivedHomReflector (hΦ : ∀ X, Z X → IsZero ((Φ ⋙ L).obj X)) :
    DA ⥤ RightModule.derivedCategory (endDGCategory C K) :=
  derivedReflectorDescent C L Φ Z q hΦ ⋙ derivedEvaluation C K

/-- `R̃` commutes with the shift by `ℤ`, as the composite of `J` and the derived evaluation. -/
noncomputable instance (hΦ : ∀ X, Z X → IsZero ((Φ ⋙ L).obj X)) :
    (derivedHomReflector C K L Φ Z q hΦ).CommShift ℤ :=
  inferInstanceAs
    ((derivedReflectorDescent C L Φ Z q hΦ ⋙ derivedEvaluation C K).CommShift ℤ)

/-- **`R̃` is exact.** It is the composite of two exact functors: the descent `J` of step 1 and
evaluation at `K` on the derived categories. -/
instance isTriangulated_derivedHomReflector
    (hΦ : ∀ X, Z X → IsZero ((Φ ⋙ L).obj X)) :
    (derivedHomReflector C K L Φ Z q hΦ).IsTriangulated :=
  inferInstanceAs
    ((derivedReflectorDescent C L Φ Z q hΦ ⋙ derivedEvaluation C K).IsTriangulated)

/-- **Restricting `R̃` along `q`** gives the reflected evaluation
`X ↦ ev_K (L (Φ X))`, read in `D(E)`. -/
noncomputable def derivedHomReflectorCompIso (hΦ : ∀ X, Z X → IsZero ((Φ ⋙ L).obj X)) :
    q ⋙ derivedHomReflector C K L Φ Z q hΦ ≅
      Φ ⋙ L ⋙ evaluationOnK.{v} C K ⋙ RightModule.derivedQ (endDGCategory C K) :=
  Functor.isoWhiskerRight (derivedReflectorDescentCompIso C L Φ Z q hΦ)
      (derivedEvaluation C K) ≪≫
    Functor.isoWhiskerLeft (Φ ⋙ L) (derivedEvaluationCompIso C K)

/-- The unit of the derived evaluation functor, transported along `Φ` and read in `D(E)`: the
natural transformation `Φ ⋙ ev_K ⋙ Q_E ⟶ Φ ⋙ L ⋙ ev_K ⋙ Q_E` obtained from `η`. For
`Φ` the differential graded Yoneda functor its source is the functor `X ↦ C(K, X)` of
[Stacks, Tag 09LE], read in `D(E)`. -/
noncomputable def derivedEvaluationUnit (η : 𝟭 (rightModuleCategory.{v} C).K ⟶ L) :
    Φ ⋙ evaluationOnK.{v} C K ⋙ RightModule.derivedQ (endDGCategory C K) ⟶
      Φ ⋙ L ⋙ evaluationOnK.{v} C K ⋙ RightModule.derivedQ (endDGCategory C K) :=
  Functor.whiskerLeft Φ (Functor.whiskerRight (evaluationReflectorUnit C K L η)
    (RightModule.derivedQ (endDGCategory C K)))

omit [L.CommShift ℤ] [L.IsTriangulated] [HasZeroObject S] [HasShift S ℤ] [Preadditive S]
  [∀ (n : ℤ), (shiftFunctor S n).Additive] [Pretriangulated S] [IsTriangulated S]
  [Φ.CommShift ℤ] [Φ.IsTriangulated] in
/-- The component of `DGCategory.derivedEvaluationUnit` at `X` is the image in `D(E)` of
`ev_K (η (Φ X))`. -/
@[simp] theorem derivedEvaluationUnit_app (η : 𝟭 (rightModuleCategory.{v} C).K ⟶ L) (X : S) :
    (derivedEvaluationUnit C K L Φ η).app X =
      (RightModule.derivedQ (endDGCategory C K)).map
        ((evaluationOnK.{v} C K).map (η.app (Φ.obj X))) := rfl

/-- **The unit `Hom_dg(K, -) ⟶ R̃ ∘ q` of step 2 of [Stacks, Tag 09LK].** The natural
transformation out of `Φ ⋙ ev_K`, read in `D(E)`, into the restriction of `R̃` along the
localization `q`: it is the unit `η` of the reflector, evaluated at `K`, pushed into `D(E)`
and composed with the identification of `q ⋙ R̃`. This is the datum step 3 compares with the
ordinary derived Hom functor. -/
noncomputable def derivedHomReflectorUnit (η : 𝟭 (rightModuleCategory.{v} C).K ⟶ L)
    (hΦ : ∀ X, Z X → IsZero ((Φ ⋙ L).obj X)) :
    Φ ⋙ evaluationOnK.{v} C K ⋙ RightModule.derivedQ (endDGCategory C K) ⟶
      q ⋙ derivedHomReflector C K L Φ Z q hΦ :=
  derivedEvaluationUnit C K L Φ η ≫ (derivedHomReflectorCompIso C K L Φ Z q hΦ).inv

/-- **The source of the unit, for the intended `Φ`.** When `Φ` is the differential graded
Yoneda functor `X ↦ h_X`, the functor `Φ ⋙ ev_K`, read in `D(E)`, is the functor
`X ↦ C(K, X)` of [Stacks, Tag 09LE] read in `D(E)`. So for that `Φ` the transformation
`DGCategory.derivedHomReflectorUnit` is a transformation out of `Hom_dg(K, -)`, which is what
step 3 compares with the ordinary derived Hom functor. -/
theorem yonedaOnK_comp_evaluationOnK_comp_derivedQ :
    (yonedaDGFunctor C).onK ⋙ evaluationOnK.{v} C K ⋙
        RightModule.derivedQ (endDGCategory C K) =
      (homDGFunctor C K).onK ⋙ RightModule.derivedQ (endDGCategory C K) := by
  rw [homDGFunctor_onK]
  rfl

end DGCategory

end
